{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-core":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-adelic-tate-bsd-reduction","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:adelic-tate-bsd-reduction","kind":"remark","title":"Adelic Fourier transform gives the BSD leading-coefficient prefactor as a local-squeeze check","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-bsd-surreal-bound","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-adelic-tate-squeeze"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-bsd-surreal-bound","tags":["adeles","tate-thesis","bsd","fourier-transform","millennium-problem","surreal","local-global"],"text":"adelic-tate-bsd-reduction.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:adelic-tate-bsd-reduction\"></a><p><strong>BSD reduction.</strong> The leading-coefficient form of BSD requires identifying the standard-real prefactor <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c</code> in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">L(\\mathcal{E}_q, s) \\asymp c \\cdot (s - 1)^r</code>. The adelic Fourier transform of <a href=\"#rem:adelic-tate-squeeze\" data-at=\"stochastic-mechanics/classical-limit-implications\">adelic Tate squeeze</a> gives the explicit formula for <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c</code> in terms of local <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\varepsilon_p</code>-adic integrals.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/stochastic-mechanics/adelic-tate-bsd-reduction.md","heading":"Remark","number":"21.5.8","page":"b/rem-adelic-tate-bsd-reduction","qa":{"counts":{"pass":59,"fail":0,"warn":1,"na":17,"unknown":0,"stale":0},"milnor":{"total":2,"notPassing":0}}}
